Single Responsibility Principle states that a class should have only one reason or responsibility to change

lets suppose that is a user service

  @service
  public class UserService{

     public void createUser(){
         //first responsibility
     }

     public void sendEmail(){
       //second responsibility
     }
     public void generateReport(){
     //third responsibility
     }
  }

  resultant output be must

  1. @service
   public class UserService{

      public void createUser(){

      }
   }
  2. @service
      public class EmailService{

         public void sendEmail(){

         }
      }
  3. @service
      public class ReportService{

              public void generateReport(){

              }
           }
//each one of them must have different responsibility

like in spring-boot architecture we declare
Controller - for handing all the http requests or request mapping (one purpose)
Service - for handling all the business logic we declare all of the business logic there
Repository - for handling all the data base operations we declared all the CRUD operations there.

//its also used in order management.

